# Building a UI

This guide builds a small, themed web page with Howdy UI, then brings part
of it to life with a component that runs on the server. It takes about
fifteen minutes and touches every part of the UI package, with links to the
page that covers each part in depth.

Howdy UI is a separate package from Howdy itself, so an app that only serves
JSON never pulls it in. It is built on two Gleam libraries:
[Lustre](https://hexdocs.pm/lustre), for writing HTML as Gleam functions,
and [Sketch](https://hexdocs.pm/sketch), for writing CSS the same way. You
do not need to know either to follow along.

## Before you start

A project made with `howdy new` already has Howdy UI installed and a themed
home page, so you can skip to the next section.

To add it to another project, put these in the `[dependencies]` section of
`gleam.toml`:

```toml
howdy_ui = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v1.0.0", path = "ui" }
lustre = ">= 5.7.0 and < 6.0.0"
```

`lustre` is listed because your own code will import it to write HTML.

## A first page

A page is a handler like any other. Build it with `howdy/ui/page`, fill the
body with components from `howdy/ui`, and answer the request with
`page.respond`:

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/page
import lustre/element.{text}

pub fn home_controller() {
  controller.new("")
  |> controller.get("/", home)
}

fn home(ctx: Context) {
  page.new("Orders")
  |> page.body([
    ui.container([], [
      ui.stack([], [
        ui.h1("Orders"),
        ui.p([text("Nothing here yet.")]),
      ]),
    ]),
  ])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

Open the page and you have a complete HTML document: a title, a viewport
tag, and a centred column with a heading and a paragraph, already styled.
There is no CSS file to link and no build step. The page carries the styles
for the components it uses.

[Pages](/docs/ui/pages) covers the document, and
[Components](/docs/ui/components) lists what `howdy/ui` offers.

## Light and dark

The page already follows your system's light or dark setting. Try switching
it and reloading. Every component reads its colours from the *theme*, and a
page offers a light and a dark one unless you say otherwise.

Let visitors choose for themselves with a toggle, and remember the choice
in a cookie:

```gleam
import howdy/cookie

fn home(ctx: Context) {
  use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system")

  page.new("Orders")
  |> page.theme(theme)
  |> page.body([
    ui.container([], [
      ui.stack([], [
        ui.h1("Orders"),
        ui.p([text("Nothing here yet.")]),
        ui.theme_toggle([text("Toggle theme")], from: "light", to: "dark"),
      ]),
    ]),
  ])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

The toggle switches the theme in the browser at once and stores the choice
in a `theme` cookie. On the next request `cookie.string_or` reads it back
and `page.theme` puts it on the document, so the page arrives in the right
theme with no flash. A visitor who has never chosen has no cookie, gets
`"system"`, which is not a theme the page offers, and so is left to their
system setting.

## Your own colours

A theme is a record. Make your own by starting from a built-in one and
changing what you want:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/theme.{Colors}

pub fn brand() -> theme.Theme {
  theme.light()
  |> theme.named("brand")
  |> theme.colors(fn(c) { Colors(..c, primary: "#0f766e", on_primary: "#fff") })
}
```

```gleam
page.new("Orders")
|> page.themes(theme.themes(default: brand(), alternatives: [theme.dark()]))
```

Every button and link on the page is now teal, without touching a single
component. [Themes](/docs/ui/themes) has the full list of what a theme can
set.

## Bringing it to life

So far the page is plain HTML. For something interactive, Howdy UI can run a
*live component*: a piece of UI whose state and logic stay on the server.
The browser sends clicks up a WebSocket and applies the changes that come
back. You write no JavaScript.

A live component is a Lustre app: a starting state, a function that updates
it for each message, and a view.

```gleam
import gleam/int
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/button.{Primary, Secondary}
import lustre
import lustre/element.{text}
import lustre/event

pub type Msg {
  Increment
  Decrement
}

pub fn counter() {
  lustre.simple(init, update, view)
}

fn init(start: Int) -> Int {
  start
}

fn update(count: Int, msg: Msg) -> Int {
  case msg {
    Increment -> count + 1
    Decrement -> count - 1
  }
}

fn view(count: Int) {
  ui.card([], [
    ui.h2("Counter"),
    ui.p([text(int.to_string(count))]),
    ui.row([], [
      ui.button(Secondary, [event.on_click(Decrement)], [text("-")]),
      ui.button(Primary, [event.on_click(Increment)], [text("+")]),
    ]),
  ])
}
```

It needs two things: a route for the socket, and a place in a page.

```gleam
import howdy/ui/live

pub fn home_controller() {
  controller.new("")
  |> controller.get("/", home)
  |> controller.get("/counter", fn(ctx) {
    live.serve(ctx, counter(), with: 0)
  })
}
```

```gleam
page.new("Orders")
|> page.live
|> page.body([
  ui.container([], [ui.h1("Orders"), live.mount("/counter")]),
])
|> page.respond(ctx)
```

`page.live` adds the small script that connects, and `live.mount` marks
where the component goes. Click the buttons and the count changes, with the
number held on the server. Open the page in two tabs and each has its own
count, because each connection gets its own copy of the component.

[Live components](/docs/ui/live-components) covers sharing one component
between every visitor, sending it messages from elsewhere, and protecting
it with a guard.

## Going further

- When the built-in components are not enough, copy one into your project
  to change it, or write your own from the same building blocks. See
  [Custom components](/docs/ui/custom-components).
- Before you put a site in front of visitors, write the CSS out as one
  cacheable file. See [Publishing](/docs/ui/publishing).
